course: dictionary
lesson: 1464
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Castle is one of them: it can be a huge stone building with towers, or a piece in a game of chess. The context tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «On day one all three built a castle, in the same spot, on top of each other, and it looked like a sandwich made by a tornado.» People build castles out of sand on the beach, so here a castle is a sandy heap, not a stone building. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence?
explain: «Her plan: the aunt does the prisoners' washing ... and he can walk out of the castle as the washerwoman.» Prisoners and washing tell you this castle is the big stone building where people are kept. Try the meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Castle is a noun: a castle, castles.
fact: A castle is a large, strong building with thick walls and towers, built long ago to protect a king, queen or lord from enemies.
fact: In chess, a castle is another name for a rook. It can also be a verb: to castle is a special move with the king and a rook.
fact: In history lessons, castles had moats, drawbridges, towers, battlements and dungeons.
fact: A sandcastle is a heap of sand shaped like a castle, built on a beach.
exercise: «The knights rode out from the castle at dawn.» What is a castle here? | A sandy heap built on a beach | *A large, strong building with thick walls and towers | A bouncy toy for children | A piece in chess
exercise: In a game of chess, Sam moves his rook. Another name for a rook is a what? | *A castle | A sandcastle | A moat | A market-place
exercise: «On the beach, Ava built a castle with towers and a moat.» What does castle mean here? | *A sandcastle, a heap of sand shaped like a building | A grim old prison in the middle of town | A chess piece | A king's throne
test: «Toad is dragged off in chains, shrieking, across the market-place ... into a grim old castle, up winding stairs.» What kind of castle is this? | A bouncy inflatable | A chess piece | A sandy heap on the beach | *A large stone building with towers and dungeons
test: Which word means the special chess move made with the king and a rook? | *To castle | To drag | To shriek | To jeer
exam: In «Your body is a castle under siege by germs too small to see», what does castle mean? | A chess piece | *Something protected by walls and guards, like a fortress | A market-place where people throw carrots | A sandcastle on the beach
